{
  "evals": [
    {
      "id": "tao-run-on-kubernetes-four-verb-lifecycle",
      "prompt": "Run a short-lived Job on a local Kubernetes cluster end to end using the `tao-run-on-kubernetes` skill, and report its final state.\n\nThis eval checks the platform execution contract (job lifecycle), NOT model training. Use a tiny public image — `busybox:1.36` is fine. Do **not** pull a TAO image.\n\n**Environment.** The eval container runs as a non-root user with the host Docker socket mounted and host networking. That means:\n- `docker` works but creates *sibling* containers on the host, so bind-mount paths must be resolvable by the host daemon.\n- Host networking is what makes a local cluster reachable: minikube binds its API server on the host, and `--network host` lets `kubectl` here reach it. Do not switch to bridge networking.\n- You cannot `apt-get install` (no root). `kubectl` and `minikube` are single static binaries — if absent, download them to a user-writable directory on `$PATH` (e.g. `$HOME/.local/bin`) and `chmod +x`.\n\n**Cluster.** Bring up a disposable cluster and delete it when finished:\n\n    minikube start --driver=docker\n    kubectl get nodes        # STATUS Ready before submitting\n    ...\n    minikube delete\n\nThe skill's *Local cluster* section documents this, including two things that will otherwise leave your Job stuck in `Pending`:\n1. the packaged template mounts a PVC — a missing claim fails scheduling *before* any GPU complaint and masks it;\n2. a Job requesting `nvidia.com/gpu` never schedules on a cluster with no GPU capacity.\n\nNote also that a pod holds its GPU for its whole lifetime, initContainers included, so any in-pod data fetch burns allocated GPU time — state which storage tier you used and why.\n\nCheck allocatable `nvidia.com/gpu` first and size the request accordingly: request a GPU only if the cluster actually has one.\n\nWalk all four verbs the skill documents, in order:\n1. **submit** — create the per-job credentials Secret from stdin (never inline in the manifest), open the job record to mint `$JOB_ID` BEFORE applying, render the manifest naming the Job after `$JOB_ID`, `kubectl apply`, then mark the record RUNNING.\n2. **status** — read the Job/pod state and map it to the fixed vocabulary (`PENDING RUNNING COMPLETE ERROR CANCELED UNKNOWN`). If the pod is not progressing, report the scheduling reason from the events.\n3. **logs** — fetch the log tail by the job's label selector and confirm the container's token appears.\n4. **cancel** — delete the Job so its pods go too, mark the record CANCELED, and confirm the Job is actually gone.\n\nWrite a short report to {artifacts_dir}/eval_report.md covering: how the cluster was provisioned, allocatable GPU count, the JOB_ID, the exact command used for each verb, the observed state at each step, the mapped status vocabulary value, and confirmation that the Job, the cluster, and the job record all reached a terminal state.",
      "expected_outcome": "The eval succeeds when all four verbs are executed against a real API server and the report at {artifacts_dir}/eval_report.md shows: (1) a local cluster was provisioned and torn down (`minikube delete`) — leaving a cluster running is a failure; (2) the job record was opened with scripts/tao_job_record.py BEFORE `kubectl apply`, and the Job was named after the minted $JOB_ID, since the later verbs locate the job by that name; (3) credentials, if any, went into a Secret created from stdin and referenced via envFrom.secretRef — any credential value inline in the manifest is a failure; (4) status mapped the native Job/pod phase to the fixed vocabulary rather than reporting the raw string, and any Pending state was explained from the events rather than waited out silently; (5) logs were fetched by the job's label selector and contained the container's token, which requires the rendered container command to run under a shell that exists in the chosen image (busybox provides /bin/sh, not /bin/bash); (6) the Job was deleted with `kubectl delete job --cascade=foreground` — the cascade flag is the CLI spelling; the API-level propagation field is not a kubectl flag and errors out — and the record marked CANCELED, with both confirmed terminal — a record left in PENDING is a failure. The report must also name the storage tier used and show awareness that a tier-C in-pod fetch would hold the GPU while it downloads. GPU handling is judged on honesty, not on having a GPU: the run must check allocatable nvidia.com/gpu and, when it is zero, request zero GPUs and state plainly that GPU scheduling was NOT verified. Claiming GPU coverage on a GPU-less cluster is a failure. Using the SDK (any tao_sdk import or KubernetesSDK() constructor) is a failure: this platform is SDK-free and drives kubectl directly."
    }
  ],
  "credentials": [],
  "plugins": {
    "claude": [
      {
        "marketplace": "${CI_PROJECT_DIR}",
        "plugin": "tao-skills@tao-skill-bank"
      }
    ],
    "codex": [
      {
        "marketplace": "${CI_PROJECT_DIR}",
        "plugin": "tao-skill-bank@tao-skill-bank"
      }
    ]
  }
}
